  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}=2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.$ Given that the point $(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate ${y}^{\u2033}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}.$\n\n${y}^{\u2033}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}=$  \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{\u2032})^{\u2032} & =(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)^{\u2032} \\\\ {y}^{\u2033} & =(2{y}^{2})^{\u2032}+(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)^{\u2032} \\\\ {y}^{\u2033} & =4y{y}^{\u2032}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute $(2{y}^{2})^{\u2032}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{\u2032}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2033} & =4y{y}^{\u2032}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{\u2033} & =4y(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{\u2033} & =8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2033}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} & =(8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+\\frac{1}{x})\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =8(-1)^{3}+4(-1)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)+\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$",
